 ## Overview
-Geometry Shader Voxelization converts polygonal meshes (`.obj`, `.ply`) into a discrete 3D occupancy or color grid by rasterizing triangles directly into a 3D texture using an OpenGL Geometry Shader [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently filling a 3D grid using standard GPU hardware rasterizers, which are optimized for 2D screens [S4]. Unlike [GPU Mesh Voxelization via Axis-Aligned Projection](2561-gpu-mesh-voxelization-via-axis-aligned-projection.md) which explicitly selects the optimal projection axis per triangle to maximize coverage, this method uses generic 3D rasterization logic or swizzles geometry to align with the XY plane while checking overlaps in all three dimensions (XY, YZ, ZX) [S3, unofficial]. It differs from [GPU Voxelization Using OpenGL Image Atomics](2544-gpu-voxelization-using-opengl-image-atomics.md) by typically relying on the geometry shader's ability to emit multiple primitives or iterate through grid cells, rather than pure atomic accumulation without depth-based projection [S4].
+Geometry Shader Voxelization converts polygonal meshes (`.obj`, `.ply`) into a discrete 3D occupancy or color grid by rasterizing triangles directly into a 3D texture using an OpenGL Geometry Shader [S3, unofficial]. It projects each triangle to cover grid cells at a specified resolution and determines occupied voxels by checking overlaps in all three dimensions (XY, YZ, ZX) [S3, unofficial]. Unlike [GPU Mesh Voxelization via Axis-Aligned Projection](2561-gpu-mesh-voxelization-via-axis-aligned-projection.md) which explicitly selects the optimal projection axis per triangle to maximize coverage, this method swizzles geometry to align with the XY plane while iterating through bounding boxes to verify 3D overlaps [S3, unofficial]. It differs from [GPU Voxelization Using OpenGL Image Atomics](2544-gpu-voxelization-using-opengl-image-atomics.md) by relying on the geometry shader's ability to emit multiple primitives and iterate through grid cells, rather than pure atomic accumulation without depth-based projection [S3, unofficial].
 
